---
import GuideLayout from '@layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import ReferenceBlock from '@components/ReferenceBlock.astro';
import { config } from './config';
import { toolPath, categoryPath } from '@lib/paths';
import { categories } from '@data/categories';
import type { EcosystemEntry } from '@data/types';

interface Props {
  title: string;
  description: string;
  readMinutes: number;
  updatedAt: string;
  entry: EcosystemEntry;
}

const props = Astro.props;
const category = categories.find(c => c.slug === config.categorySlug)!;
const toolHref = toolPath({ slug: config.slug, segment: category.segment });
---

<GuideLayout
  {...props}
  toolSlug={config.slug}
  toolName={config.name}
  toolCategorySlug={config.categorySlug}
>
  <section id="quick-answer">
    <h2>Quick Answer <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      A reading time calculator is a read time estimator that shows the time to read any piece of text
      by dividing the word count by a standard reading speed. At 200 words per minute (the commonly cited average for adult silent reading) a 1,000-word article gives an estimated reading time of about 5 minutes,
      the "5 min read" label you see on blogs.
    </p>
    <a href={toolHref} class="cta-link">Try The Reading Time Calculator →</a>
  </section>

  <section id="why-reading-time-matters">
    <h2>Why Reading Time Matters <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Blog posts and articles.</strong> Displaying reading time at the top of an article
      sets reader expectations. Readers who know a piece will take 4 minutes are more likely to
      commit than those who face an unknown length. Studies on Medium and similar platforms find
      that articles in the 6 to 7 minute reading range receive the highest engagement.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Presentations and speeches.</strong> Speaking aloud is roughly 30 to 40 percent
      slower than silent reading. A 500-word script takes about 2.5 minutes to read silently
      but 3 to 4 minutes to deliver as a speech. Use it as a presentation timer to verify your
      script fits the allotted slot before you rehearse.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Email and professional communication.</strong> Long emails with no reading time signal
      are often skimmed or deferred. Knowing a draft will take more than 3 minutes to read
      is a useful editorial signal: consider cutting, summarizing, or splitting it.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Content planning.</strong> Matching reading time to user intent improves content
      quality. A how-to guide targeting a quick task should stay under 3 minutes. A deep-dive
      analysis for a specialist audience can justify 12 to 15 minutes. Reading time makes the
      tradeoff visible.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="how-reading-time-works">
    <h2>How Reading Time Is Calculated <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Here is how it works: this tool divides the word count by a reading speed baseline to produce an estimated duration.
      The default baseline is 200 words per minute: the conservative end of the research range for
      adult silent reading of prose. For example, a 600-word email is estimated at 3 minutes
      (600 ÷ 200 = 3).
    </p>
    <p>
      Speaking time uses a separate baseline of 130 words per minute, reflecting the slower pace
      of deliberate speech delivery compared to silent reading.
    </p>
    <p>
      This tool is free and runs online entirely in your browser. Your text stays on your device,
      protecting your privacy: nothing is uploaded to a server, and results appear instantly
      as you type or paste.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="words-to-minutes">
    <h2>How Long Does It Take to Read 1,000 Words? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      About 5 minutes. Reading time is the word count divided by words per minute, and at the
      200 WPM average the common lengths work out like this:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>500 words:</strong> 2.5 minutes to read, about 3.8 minutes to say aloud.</li>
      <li><strong>1,000 words:</strong> 5 minutes to read, about 7.7 minutes aloud.</li>
      <li><strong>1,500 words:</strong> 7.5 minutes to read, about 11.5 minutes aloud.</li>
      <li><strong>2,000 words:</strong> 10 minutes to read, about 15.4 minutes aloud.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The speaking figures use 130 words per minute, a steady presentation pace. So for a 10-minute
      talk, you need a script of about 1,300 words.
    </p>
    <p>
      This tool rounds both estimates to the nearest whole minute and never shows less than 1 minute.
      For example, 500 words reads as 3 min and speaks as 4 min. That is the same convention as the "5 min read"
      labels on blogs.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="reading-speed-research">
    <h2>What Research Says About Reading Speed <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Multiple studies place average adult silent reading speed at 200 to 250 words per minute
      for prose comprehension. Technical and scientific material is read more slowly (typically 100 to 150 words per minute) because of unfamiliar vocabulary and denser structure.
      Skimming can exceed 700 words per minute but with substantially lower retention.
    </p>
    <p>
      For example, a 2,000-word technical article about database internals might take an expert
      8 minutes but a newcomer 15 minutes. Reading time estimates assume a general prose baseline;
      treat them as approximate guides rather than precise measurements.
    </p>
    <ReferenceBlock type="note" heading="Reading speed varies significantly by reader and content">
      <p>
        Reading speed is not the same for every reader. The 200 wpm figure is a population average.
        Individual reading speeds range from 150 to 400 wpm depending on reading habits, familiarity
        with the subject, and content density. Use the estimate as a rough guide: actual reading time
        will vary.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>

  <section id="common-mistakes">
    <h2>Common Mistakes <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Using reading time as the sole quality metric">
      <p>
        A common mistake is treating reading time as a target rather than a signal. Avoid padding
        articles with filler sentences to hit a "7-minute read" threshold. Readers notice thin
        content, and search engines penalise low-value padding. Use reading time to spot articles
        that are surprisingly short or long relative to their stated scope.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Applying reading time to non-prose content">
      <p>
        Reading time calculators are calibrated for prose: continuous narrative text. Images and code
        change the real time, so do not apply the estimate to code blocks, tables, listicles, or
        heavily formatted reference pages. A page that is 40% code and 60% prose will show an inflated
        reading time because the word counter counts code tokens as words, while an image-heavy page
        reads faster than its word count suggests.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>

  <section id="why-calculators-differ">
    <h2>Why Do Reading Time Calculators Differ? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Paste the same article into three calculators and you can get three answers. The arithmetic is
      identical. However, the assumptions are not:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Words per minute:</strong> some tools use 200, others 238 or 250. Medium has said it uses 265 and adds time for each image.</li>
      <li><strong>What counts as a word:</strong> hyphenated terms, numbers, URLs and code can count as one word, several, or none.</li>
      <li><strong>Rounding:</strong> nearest minute, always up, or seconds shown in full. A 2.4-minute post can read as 2, 3, or 2 min 24 s.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      None of them is wrong, so pick one calculator and use it consistently. If your real limit is a
      number of words or characters rather than reader attention, a word counter or character counter
      is the better tool.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="related-tools">
    <p>
      <strong>Related tools:</strong> explore all text analysis utilities in the
      <a href={categoryPath(category)}>Text Utilities</a> collection,
      including Word Counter, Character Counter, Sentence Counter, and Paragraph Counter.
    </p>
  </section>

</GuideLayout>

<style>
  .cta-link {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    height: var(--touch-target);
    padding-inline: var(--space-5);
    margin-top: var(--space-5);
    background: var(--color-accent);
    color: var(--color-text-inverse);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
    font-size: var(--text-base);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: var(--transition-color);
  }
  .cta-link:hover { opacity: 0.9; text-decoration: none; }
</style>
